 ## Background
-The standard Marching Cubes algorithm was introduced by William E. Lorensen and Harvey E. Cline in 1987 [10327-lookup-table-and-ambiguity-handling]. The lookup table approach became the standard implementation due to its efficiency. Subsequent work addressed the topological ambiguities inherent in the original 256-case table, leading to variants like the Improved Marching Cubes algorithm [9090-improved-marching-cubes-algorithm] and symmetry-based optimizations [869-symmetry-mapping]. The Watt & Watt implementation [7787-watt-watt-marching-cubes] further optimized the algorithm for large-scale data processing by addressing memory and I/O constraints.
